"νύμφ-η, ἡ ( Ep. voc. νύμφα^ Il. 3.130 , Od. 4.743 ( Aeol. acc. to Choerob. in Theod. 1.304 ) ; later also as nom., AP 14.43 ; but Dor. νύμφα—), young wife, bride, Il. 18.492 , Hdt. 4.172 ; ν. ἄγεσθαι Ar. Pl. 529 ; Ἀελίοιο Pi. O. 7.14 ; opp. νυμφίος, Pl. Lg. 783e ; opp. παρθένος, Praxill. 5 , Com.Adesp. 1215 ; always relatively young, as Iris calls Helen, or as Eurycleia calls Penelope, νύμφα φίλη Il. 3.130 , Od. 4.743 , cf. E. Med. 150 , Andr. 140 (both lyr.). marriageable maiden, Il. 9.560 , Hes. Th. 298 . daughter-in-law, LXX 1 Ki. 4.19 , Ev.Matt. 10.35 . young girl, πενταέτης ν. IG 14.2040.2 . Nymph or goddess of lower rank, θεαὶ Νύμφαι Il. 24.616 , cf. Hes. Th. 130 , Fr. 171.5 , al., IG 12(8).358 (Thasos, V B.C.) ; N. κοῦραι Διὸς αἰγιόχοιο Od. 6.105 ; N. ἅλιαι S. Ph. 1470 (anap. ; cf. Ναϊάς, Νηρηΐς) ; N. Ὀρεστιάδες, Ὀρειάδες, Il. 6.420 , Bion 1.19 , cf. Ar. Av. 1098 (lyr.) ; N. Μελίαι Hes. Th. 187 , cf. Ἀδρυάδες, Ἁμαδρυάδες, Δρυάδες ; N. ὑάδες, ὑδριάδες, Id. Fr. 180 , Porph. Antr. 18 ; N. λειμωνιάδες, πετραῖαι, S. Ph. 1454 (anap.), E. El. 805 . esp. of springs, ὀνομάζεσθαι τὰς πηγὰς N. Ath. 11.465a , cf. Lib. Or. 11.28 : hence, poetically, water, AP 9.258 ( Antiphan. ), 331 ( Mel. ), cf. Plu. 2.147f . in mystical theology, Ζεὺς ἄμβροτος ἔπλετο ν. Orph. Fr. 21a4 . applied to souls seeking birth, Porph. Antr. 18 ; cf. νυμφεύω. doll, puppet, AP 6.274 ( Pers. ), Jul. Caes. 332d . young bee or wasp, in the pupa stage, Arist. HA 551b2 , 555a3 . winged male of the ant, Hsch. kind of mollusc, Speusipp. ap. Ath. 3.105b . point of the ploughshare, Poll. 1.252 , Procl.ad Hes. Op. 425 . hollow between the under-lip and chin, Ruf. Onom. 42 , Poll. 2.90 , Hsch. depression on the shoulder of horses, Hippiatr. 26 . opening rosebud, Phot. clitoris, Ruf. Onom. 112 , Gal. UP 15.3 . niche, Callix. 2 ."